wcd-semantic vendors code from the UVA Wikipedia Citations Database
(https://github.com/scatter-llc/UVAWikipediaCitationsDatabase), a UVA School
of Data Science capstone project (Spring 2026) carried out for Scatter LLC,
at commit 29f3ae319d54c753843f3e0b54a1a85c32e872b0.

Provenance (upstream path → module here; only the pure-logic half of each
upstream module is carried over, the DuckDB loading/writing code is not):

  src/citation_normalize/reftag_normalizer.py   → reftag.py
  src/citation_normalize/template_normalizer.py → template.py
  src/wcd_extract/citation_extractor.py         → content.py (per-ref parsing), signals.py (flags)
  src/citation_matching/ref_tag_matching.py     → match_reftag.py, unionfind.py
  src/citation_matching/template_matching.py    → match_template.py
  src/citation_matching/cross_type_merger.py    → merge_types.py
  src/citation_matching/cross_page_matcher.py   → match_global.py, scoring.py

Rules for this package:
  * Logic in the vendored functions is kept verbatim. The golden tests in
    tests/ were generated by running the upstream code at the commit above
    on fixed inputs; a change that alters their output must be deliberate,
    documented in the commit, and the golden files regenerated.
  * Additions (new identifier signals, new entry points such as match_page)
    are marked as such in the module docstrings and tested separately.

Documented deviations from upstream logic:
  1. reftag.py `_ID_URL_PARAMS` is an ordered tuple (url first) instead of a
     set; upstream's set iteration made the extracted URL of a cite template
     inside a ref depend on the Python hash seed. The golden generator applies
     the same tuple to upstream before recording.
